+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this helpful comment. We agree that the original Introduction mixed background, literature and objectives, and stated the contributions before the literature that should motivate them. We have reorganized the two sections as follows. (1) The Introduction is now limited to the forecasting problem, the development of machine-learning weather prediction and the regional setting and target; its literature paragraphs on uncertainty estimation and interpretability have been moved to Related Work, and the former Section~1.1 has been removed. (2) Related Work is organized into five problem-oriented subsections: precipitation data products (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_data}); machine-learning forecasting and model comparison (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_ml}); probabilistic forecasting and reliability (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_prob}); interpretability and predictor relevance (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_interp}); and research gaps and objectives (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:gaps_objectives}). (3) Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_ml}--\ref{manuscript-sec:rw_interp} each end with an explicitly stated evaluation gap derived from the cited studies: (i) published comparisons differ in target, spatial support, lead time and verification data \citep{rahman2022rainfall,adewoyin2021tru,singh2021deep,otero2023intercomparison}, so a common-setting comparison with strong non-deep baselines \citep{grinsztajn2022tree} and sensitivity to random initialization is needed; (ii) aggregate scores do not describe heavy-precipitation behaviour \citep{adewoyin2021tru,otero2023intercomparison}; (iii) overall probabilistic performance need not imply upper-tail reliability, which proper and threshold-weighted scoring rules can assess \citep{gneiting2007proper,gneiting2011weighted}; and (iv) correlation, attribution and ablation answer different questions \citep{lundberg2017unified}. Gap (ii) does not claim that tail evaluation is absent from the